  "nameStory": "The Salmon Amanita gets its name from its distinctive salmon-pink to orange-pink coloring that develops on the cap, reminiscent of the rosy flesh of salmon fish. This beautiful peachy-orange hue makes it stand out among the many white and brown amanitas that populate New England's forests.",
  "description": "The Salmon Amanita is a medium-sized mushroom with a distinctive peachy-salmon colored cap that can fade to yellowish or whitish toward the edges. Like all amanitas, it has white gills underneath the cap, a sturdy white stem, and grows from a cup-like structure at the base called a volva. These mushrooms typically reach 3-6 inches across the cap when fully mature, making them quite noticeable when fruiting in mixed forest settings.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap",
      "description": "Salmon-pink to peachy-orange color, often fading to yellowish-white toward the edges. The surface is smooth and may have white patches or scales from the universal veil remnants.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem",
      "description": "White and sturdy, emerging from a distinctive cup-like volva at the base. The stem may have a ring around the upper portion and feels firm when gently squeezed.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills",
      "description": "Pure white gills that are free from the stem (don't attach directly to it). They remain white and don't change color when bruised.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Volva",
      "description": "Look for the characteristic cup-like structure at the stem base - this is the hallmark of amanitas. It may be partially buried in soil or leaf litter.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Print",
      "description": "Produces a white spore print when the cap is placed gill-side down on dark paper overnight.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Safety Note",
      "description": "NEVER eat any amanita mushroom - this family includes some of the most deadly mushrooms in North America. Edibility of this species is unknown, but it's not worth the risk.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found scattered or in small groups on the forest floor in mixed woodlands throughout New England, particularly where alder trees are present. They prefer rich, moist soils and are often found in areas with both hardwoods and conifers, forming mycorrhizal relationships with tree roots.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "No fruiting occurs during spring months. Look for last year's dried remains that may persist from previous fall fruiting.",
    "summer": "Peak fruiting season typically begins in mid to late summer, especially after periods of warm rain. Fresh specimens are most common from July through August.",
    "fall": "Continued fruiting into early fall, particularly in September. Cool, moist conditions can extend the fruiting season, but specimens become less common as temperatures drop.",
    "winter": "No active fruiting in winter. Any remains from fall will be dried, blackened, or completely decomposed by winter's end."
  },
  "funFact": "This beautiful mushroom was first scientifically described in 1920 based on specimens collected in Springfield, New Hampshire, making it a true New England native that was literally 'born' in our region for the scientific world!",
  "confusionNotes": "Most commonly confused with other colorful amanitas like Jackson's Slender Caesar or American Blusher, but the salmon-pink coloration is quite distinctive. Remember: if it has the amanita volva (cup) at the base and white gills, treat it as potentially dangerous regardless of the pretty colors.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "The species name 'wellsii' honors Professor H.L. Wells, who studied this mushroom before it was officially described by science. This reflects the important role that dedicated amateur naturalists play in advancing our understanding of fungi - many species are first noticed by careful observers before scientists give them official names.",
  "compareHints": "Unlike the red-capped Fly Agaric with its white spots, or the orange Jackson's Slender Caesar which lacks a ring, the Salmon Amanita has that unique peachy-salmon coloration combined with both a ring and volva. It's less vibrant than Jackson's Slender Caesar but more colorful than the pale Yellow Patches, occupying a distinctive middle ground in the colorful amanita spectrum.",
